<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Tututepec</h1><p class="st-meta">1083 CE – 1522 CE (approx.) · capital Tututepec</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The powerful Mixtec kingdom of the Pacific coast of Oaxaca, founded under Lord Eight Deer, which resisted the Aztecs to the end.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Tututepec, Yucu Dzaa in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mixtecs">Mixtec</a>, became a royal seat in the late 11th century, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eight-deer-jaguar-claw">Lord Eight Deer Jaguar Claw</a> established himself there around 1083 and used it as a base for conquests. It grew into the largest state of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mixteca-costa">Mixteca Costa</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Tututepec controlled a large coastal territory with many subject towns, drawing tribute in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacao">cacao</a>, gold and shells. Its rulers fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zapotecs">Zapotecs</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire</a>, which never fully conquered it. Archaeological survey shows a city of perhaps 20,000 people with outlying settlements across the lower Río Verde valley.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1522 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-de-alvarado">Pedro de Alvarado</a> conquered Tututepec and seized its gold. The kingdom became a Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/encomienda">encomienda</a>, and its native lords survived as caciques in the colonial era.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1083 CE – 1522 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:81.25%;width:8.74%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1083 CE – 1522 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tututepec</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Lord Eight Deer Jaguar Claw</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Mixtec religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Mixtec</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Yucu Dzaa, Kingdom of Tututepec</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1302&amp;focus=tututepec" aria-label="Tututepec on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2020 1579 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Tututepec lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2340 1835L2356 1793M2340 1835L2321 1717"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2356 1793h0M2321 1717h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2340 1835h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2340 1835h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1302&amp;focus=tututepec">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
